{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "target": "ES2022",
    // Rule 3 (05-03): "NodeNext" required an `exports` map to resolve any
    // package subpath. Next.js's own packages (e.g. `next/navigation`, the
    // first bare `next/*` subpath import in this codebase) ship without an
    // `exports` field, so `tsc --noEmit` failed with "Cannot find module
    // 'next/navigation'" even though the file resolves fine at runtime
    // (Vite/Next's bundler don't consult these fields for transpilation).
    // Switched to "esnext"/"bundler" -- Next.js's own documented tsconfig
    // recommendation for this exact project shape -- which is a strict
    // superset: every existing relative "*.js"-suffixed import (the
    // NodeNext convention already used throughout this codebase) still
    // resolves identically under "bundler", it just additionally supports
    // Next's exports-map-free package layout.
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    // Rule 1 (04-01): the plan specified "preserve", but Next.js 16.2.10's
    // own `next build` tsconfig writer mandatorily overwrites this to
    // "react-jsx" on every run ("next.js uses the React automatic
    // runtime") -- verified by two consecutive `next build` runs. Setting
    // "preserve" here would just be silently reverted on the next build,
    // producing a permanently dirty working tree. Locking to the value
    // Next.js itself requires.
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "lib": [
      "ES2022",
      "DOM",
      "DOM.Iterable"
    ],
    "incremental": true,
    "plugins": [
      {
        "name": "next"
      }
    ],
    "allowJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    // Rule 3 (06-01): shadcn's CLI init requires a pre-existing `@/*` path
    // alias to detect this repo's import-alias convention before it will
    // run at all ("Could not find valid path aliases... Configure path
    // aliases in tsconfig.json... then run init again" -- confirmed live
    // this session). Adding it manually, additive only -- coexists with
    // the existing relative ".js"-suffixed imports (an orthogonal
    // extension-resolution mechanism, see next.config.ts's
    // `resolve.extensionAlias`), scoped to new shadcn-adjacent code only
    // per 06-PATTERNS.md Pattern 1.
    //
    // Rule 3 (06-01): typescript@7.0.2 (tsgo) removed the `baseUrl` option
    // entirely ("Option 'baseUrl' has been removed... Use '\"paths\":
    // {\"*\": [\"./*\"]}' instead" -- confirmed live this session running
    // `npx tsc --noEmit`). `paths` entries are resolved relative to the
    // tsconfig file itself without `baseUrl` under tsgo, so `"./src/*"`
    // below still resolves correctly with `baseUrl` omitted.
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  "include": [
    "src",
    "app",
    "next-env.d.ts",
    // Rule 3 (04-01): tsc must see this file's `@testing-library/jest-dom/vitest`
    // side-effect import to pick up its global `Assertion` matcher-type
    // augmentation (e.g. `toBeInTheDocument`) when type-checking any *.test.tsx
    // file under src/ -- otherwise `tsc --noEmit` fails on every jest-dom matcher.
    "vitest.setup.ts",
    ".next/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next/dev/types/**/*.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules"
  ]
}
